From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 22:44:03 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Collect M4 Pro and M4 Max Wi-Fi from the installed macOS

Every M4 Pro and M4 Max install since Alpha 17 came up without Wi-Fi.
Their BCM4388 rev C2 needs brcmfmac4388c2-pcie.apple,<board> firmware,
and the package had none, for two reasons.

Both collectors dropped the whole C-4388__s-C2 directory, a rule from
Asahi for a broken 14.6.1 IPSW that cost M1 to M3 nothing. And the
firmware was read only from the Recovery image, whose 26.6.2 (25G83)
C2 directory holds aruba NVRAM and regulatory data alone: its aruba.trx
names a paradise.trx the image does not ship. The installed system
volume ships every board, with hostomel.trx and mriya.trx naming
guam.trx (sha256 8b18acb6..., the file the dev J616s install was hand
fixed with).

Both collectors now convert C2 like any other directory. The Python one
skips a dangling alias by itself instead of the directory around it;
the Swift snapshot already left such links out.

The native firmware step reads the Mac's Wi-Fi board from the ADT wlan
node's module-instance and builds Wi-Fi from the running system's
sealed, read-only /usr/share/firmware/wifi when that tree carries the
board's firmware, falling back to Recovery otherwise. The fallback
archive keeps the tree the package came from, so a re-extraction on
Linux gives the same set. On J614s, J614c, J616s and J616c the step now
refuses a package that lacks the board's .bin, .clm_blob or .txt for
the board or any antenna SKU, or its Bluetooth .bin and .ptb, rather
than installing a Mac without radios.

AuroraFirmwareCheck radio ROOT MACHINE reports a tree's board files and
anything missing. Against this host's 25G83 trees: the system volume is
complete for j616s (hostomel) and j614s (mriya); Recovery lacks every
C2 Wi-Fi file for both, while its C2 Bluetooth is complete.

Tests: conformance of each M4 Pro/Max board's package, Recovery-shaped
and system-shaped fixtures in Swift and Python, and the vendor firmware
step's source choice and fallback archive.

(cherry picked from commit 34f90a5ab71019b84fb7e586d71e2c90e3fef133)
[j604: aurora-silicon/aurora-installer 34f90a5 limited to asahi_firmware/wifi.py; the Swift
 collector, stub.py and the native firmware step have no counterpart in asahi-alarm-installer.
 Applies without conflict on v0.9.2 + 0001. Without it a re-extraction on Linux (asahi-fwupdate)
 drops every brcmfmac4388c2-pcie.apple,* file, which the J604 Wi-Fi (BCM4388 C2) needs.]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 asahi_firmware/wifi.py | 11 ++++++++---
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/asahi_firmware/wifi.py b/asahi_firmware/wifi.py
index af5abee..55f8519 100644
--- a/asahi_firmware/wifi.py
+++ b/asahi_firmware/wifi.py
@@ -45,9 +45,6 @@ class WiFiFWCollection(object):
                 dirnames.remove("perf")
             if "assert" in dirnames:
                 dirnames.remove("assert")
-            # remove broken firmware dir in 14.6.1 IPSW
-            if "C-4388__s-C2" in dirnames:
-                dirnames.remove("C-4388__s-C2")
             subpath = os.path.relpath(dirpath, source_path)
             for name in sorted(filenames):
                 if not any(name.endswith("." + i) for i in self.EXTMAP):
@@ -59,6 +56,14 @@ class WiFiFWCollection(object):
                     continue
                 path = os.path.join(dirpath, name)
                 relpath = os.path.join(subpath, name)
+                # Some Recovery images ship aliases whose target is absent
+                # (25G83's C-4388__s-C2/aruba.trx names a missing
+                # paradise.trx). Such a link carries no firmware: skip it,
+                # like a file the image lacks, and keep the rest of its
+                # directory.
+                if not os.path.exists(path):
+                    log.info(f"Skipping dangling firmware alias {relpath}")
+                    continue
                 if not name.endswith(".txt"):
                     name = "P-" + name
                 idpath, ext = os.path.join(subpath, name).rsplit(".", 1)
